<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Well of Moses</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1395–1405</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">Well</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> is a monumental sculpture by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claus-sluter">Claus Sluter</a> and his workshop, made in 1395–1405 for the cloister of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charterhouse-of-champmol">Charterhouse of Champmol</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dijon">Dijon</a>. Its hexagonal base, carved with six life-size Old Testament prophets led by Moses, once supported a Calvary group, of which only fragments survive. It is a masterpiece of late medieval realism.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/well-of-moses.jpg" alt="Well of Moses" width="320" height="503" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Benjamin Smith · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dijon_-_Puits_de_Mo%C3%AFse_-_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
